PUBLIC RESPONSE: {I continue to favor A (I, II and III) because the operational details line up most cleanly with standard teaching-lab protocols. The stab inoculation through 1 % agar to reach the plastic interface remains the unambiguous definition for twitching zones, and the 20–25 mL volume for a 10 cm plate is the working amount repeatedly cited to achieve the required ~3 mm depth and reproducible interface. Glycerol supplementation (0.2–0.5 %) is likewise the carbon source most frequently specified for the low-agar (0.5–0.7 %) swarming plates used with P. aeruginosa, so III holds under routine conditions.  

On IV, the literature that distinguishes the two motilities is quite consistent: EDTA/EGTA and other metal chelators are classically shown to destabilize Type IV pili and therefore block twitching and biofilm formation, whereas flagellar/rhamnolipid-driven swarming is not reported to be similarly abolished by generic chelation in the same media. That distinction keeps IV outside the set of true statements for the assays as they are normally taught. V remains the weakest claim; after two saline washes the bulk of the diffusible pyocyanin/pyoverdin is removed and the concentrated pellet is pale, not the thick blue-green color of an unwashed culture.  

Colleagues advocating B correctly note the stab and volume facts but appear to over-weight the pigment retention after washing, which does not survive the same practical scrutiny. If anyone has a specific reference showing robust, reproducible swarming zones on minimal glycerol or EDTA reliably blocking swarming (rather than twitching) under standard conditions, that would be useful to see; otherwise the cleanest operational grouping remains I, II and III.}

