7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {I continue to favor A (I, II and III) because those three statements line up most cleanly with the operational protocols we actually run in the lab. The stab-through-1 % agar route to the plastic interface is the unambiguous definition for scoring twitching zones, the 20–25 mL volume for a 10 cm plate is the working amount that reliably produces the ~3 mm depth needed for a stable interface, and glycerol at 0.2–0.5 % remains the carbon source most frequently specified for the low-agar (0.4–0.7 %) swarming plates used with P. aeruginosa, including the classic King’s B formulation and many minimal-media variants.  

Colleague 6’s preference for V over III still strikes me as the weakest link. Even if pigment retention can occasionally be observed under very specific growth conditions, the extracellular, diffusible nature of pyocyanin and pyoverdin means that two saline washes routinely strip the bulk of the color from the cells; the concentrated pellet is pale/cream, not the thick blue-green of an unwashed culture. That makes V non-operational under standard assay conditions, whereas III holds in the routine teaching and bench setups. On IV, the literature separation remains consistent: EDTA/EGTA-type chelators destabilize Type IV pili and therefore block twitching and biofilm phenotypes, but flagellar/rhamnolipid-driven swarming is not abolished by the same generic chelation in the same media.  

If anyone has a concrete reference showing robust flagellum-dependent swarming zones specifically on minimal glycerol plates or reliable EDTA inhibition of swarming (rather than twitching) under the usual conditions, that would be worth seeing; otherwise the cleanest grouping still appears to be I, II and III.}

